PatchClosure-Bench repro -- django-openredirect-2015-2317-2017-7233
CVE-2015-2317 (V1=1.8.0, incomplete fix, TARGET) -> CVE-2017-7233 (V2=1.8.18, complete)
run_mode = real-app-http  (NO source-lift; the prior lifted/ harness was removed and rebuilt)

WHAT RUNS
  - REAL published Django installed per profile via pip on python:2.7-slim:
        V0 = Django 1.7.5   (pip Django==1.7.5)   image pcb-django-issafeurl:1.7.5   port 43101
        V1 = Django 1.8.0   (pip Django==1.8.0)   image pcb-django-issafeurl:1.8.0   port 43102   [TARGET, GIVEN]
        V2 = Django 1.8.18  (pip Django==1.8.18)  image pcb-django-issafeurl:1.8.18  port 43103
  - REAL Django project app/ (manage.py + proj/{settings,urls,wsgi}.py), served by gunicorn 19.10.0.
  - The /login view `go` is a genuine redirect view: it calls Django's OWN
        django.utils.http.is_safe_url(next, host=request.get_host())
    and returns HttpResponseRedirect(next) iff approved, else HttpResponseRedirect('/').
    NO Django source is copied; is_safe_url is exercised straight out of the installed package
    (the import path django.utils.http.is_safe_url is identical across 1.7.5/1.8.0/1.8.18).

EXTERNAL HTTP TRIGGER (oracle.py run_probe)
  GET /login?next=<percent-encoded vector>  with header  Host: app.local
  -> the oracle reads the Location header of the REAL 302 from the running container (no -L).
  request.get_host() resolves to 'app.local' (ALLOWED_HOSTS='*'), so is_safe_url's `host` arg is
  the app's own host; the attacker host <FLAG>.evil.example is off-origin.

VECTORS (FLAG = per-run ${PCB_FLAG} used ONLY as the attacker redirect-host label)
  ORIGINAL (CVE-2015-2317): next = \x7f//<FLAG>.evil.example          (leading DEL 0x7f, %7F-encoded)
  RESIDUAL (CVE-2017-7233): next = http://app.local\@<FLAG>.evil.example   (backslash before @)

is_safe_url behavior observed LIVE over HTTP (per-version decision = the tracked sink):
  V0 1.7.5 : NO leading-control-char check -> approves \x7f//<FLAG>... (netloc=='' , relative).
  V1 1.8.0 : CVE-2015-2317 fix `if unicodedata.category(url[0])[0]=='C': return False` -> REJECTS the
             \x7f seed; but validates only url.replace('\\','/') once, so APPROVES the \@ residual
             (normalised http://app.local/@<FLAG>... has netloc=='app.local'==host).
  V2 1.8.18: CVE-2017-7233 fix `_is_safe_url(url,host) and _is_safe_url(url.replace('\\','/'),host)`
             -> also validates the RAW url (netloc 'app.local\@<FLAG>...' != host) -> REJECTS residual;
             control-char check still REJECTS the seed.

