FAITHFULNESS NUANCE (honest, real-software behavior):
  Real Django (all 3 versions) applies django.core.handlers.base.fix_location_header, which
  absolutizes a RELATIVE Location via build_absolute_uri. Confirmed it is Django, not the server:
  a direct WSGI call (bypassing gunicorn) also returns the absolute Location, and gunicorn's raw
  wire bytes match. Effect per vector:
   - RESIDUAL (CVE-2017-7233, the benchmark TARGET): already ABSOLUTE, emitted verbatim as
     http://app.local%5C@<FLAG>.evil.example on V1 -> a GENUINELY EXTERNAL open redirect (browser
     authority = userinfo 'app.local\' + host <FLAG>.evil.example). Fully faithful.
   - SEED (CVE-2015-2317): inherently RELATIVE (the leading control char is exactly what makes
     is_safe_url see netloc=='' and approve it), so fix_location_header absolutizes it to
     http://app.local/%7F//<FLAG>.evil.example -> the FLAG surfaces in the PATH, same-origin at the
     wire. Unavoidable: any leading control char defeats scheme-relative parsing, so no control-char
     seed can survive absolutization as an external host. The seed's gate role is only to (i) prove
     V0 approves the attacker input (FLAG present) and (ii) be blocked on V1 by the control-char
     check (FLAG absent) -- both hold; the V0-vs-V1 FLAG differential is driven exactly by the
     CVE-2015-2317 fix. is_safe_url (the patched sink) is faithfully exercised over real HTTP.

DATA-QUALITY (no rejection condition triggered):
  distinct fixed_versions (1.8.0 != 1.8.18); V1 != V0 (1.8.0 != 1.7.5, different is_safe_url);
  original != residual on V1 (original blocked, residual reaches); V1 does NOT block the residual
  (V1 approves it). Genuine same-sink incomplete-fix open redirect, Variant A.

